      {
        "id": "p44-q3-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $y=27x-x^3$ is an odd function. What symmetry does its graph display?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f(-x)=-f(x)$, so the function is odd. Its graph has $180^\\circ$ rotational symmetry about the origin.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f(-x)&=27(-x)-(-x)^3\\\\f(-x)&=-27x+x^3\\\\f(-x)&=-(27x-x^3)\\\\f(-x)&=-f(x).\\end{align*}$$\nThe function is odd, so its graph has $180^\\circ$ rotational symmetry about the origin.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 23
      },
      {
        "id": "p44-q3-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Show that $y'=3(9-x^2)$ and $y''=-6x$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y'=27-3x^2=3(9-x^2)$ and $y''=-6x$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}y'&=27-3x^2\\\\y'&=3(9-x^2)\\\\y''&=-6x.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      },
      {
        "id": "p44-q3-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Find the coordinates of the stationary points. Then determine their nature, either by examining the sign of $f''(3)$ and $f''(-3)$, or by means of a table of test values of $y'$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Local maximum at $(3,54)$; local minimum at $(-3,-54)$.",
          "worked": "At stationary points, $y'=0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}3(9-x^2)&=0\\\\x^2&=9\\\\x&=\\pm3\\\\f(3)&=27(3)-3^3\\\\f(3)&=54\\\\f(-3)&=27(-3)-(-3)^3\\\\f(-3)&=-54.\\end{align*}$$\n$$\\begin{align*}f''(3)&=-18<0\\\\f''(-3)&=18>0.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $(3,54)$ is a local maximum and $(-3,-54)$ is a local minimum.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 40
      },
      {
        "id": "p44-q3-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Show, using a table of test values of $y''$, that $x=0$ is a point of inflection.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y''$ changes from positive to negative at $x=0$, so $(0,0)$ is a point of inflection.",
